      "notes": "All four claims are adequately supported by their quoted evidence. The stated claim correctly reflects the policy text. The measurable claim on 180 job categories is a minor concern \u2014 the quote from E6 does not explicitly mention RQF Level 6 or July 2025 (those details come from E5), but the 180-job-category exclusion is directly quoted and the broader context is not materially overstated. The two projected claims are appropriately tiered and the quotes entail the claims. The mixed direction is genuinely supported by competing evidenced claims: protective wage-floor effect (stated rationale, coherent) vs. labour-market disruption in shortage sectors (E14, E17 referenced in rationale). The rationale references E17 (90% care sector drop) in text but it does not appear as a formal claim \u2014 this is a gap but not blocking since it is cited in sources and used illustratively. No tier blurring, no party bias, no false balance \u2014 both sides have real evidence behind them. Magnitude 'moderate' and 'this-parliament' horizon are plausible given the scale of affected sectors."
